 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Specific Model Architectures](8585-specific-model-architectures.md)
-Specific Model Architectures define the neural network topologies, data structures, and optimization strategies used to represent and optimize the radiance field $F_\Theta(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) = (\sigma, \mathbf{c})$. While the canonical NeRF uses a single deep MLP, this family encompasses methods that modify the network topology (e.g., multi-space decomposition, tensor grids), introduce auxiliary networks for specific tasks (e.g., depth estimation, instance segmentation), or alter the input/output mappings to support dynamic scenes, relighting, or single-view synthesis. These architectures set themselves apart from standard NeRF by addressing specific limitations such as computational cost, geometric ambiguity, lack of semantic understanding, or the inability to handle dynamic content.
+Defines neural network topologies and optimization strategies for the radiance field. Sets itself apart by modifying network topology (e.g., multi-space decomposition, tensor grids) or introducing auxiliary networks for tasks like depth estimation or instance segmentation. Choose when computational cost, geometric ambiguity, or dynamic content handling requires architectural changes beyond the standard MLP.
 
 ### [Scene Representation and Partitioning](9444-scene-representation-and-partitioning.md)
-This family of methods addresses the scalability limits of single-network NeRFs by decomposing the scene into multiple, smaller representations (submodules, blocks, or fields) that are optimized and queried independently. While the parent [Scene Representation and Architecture](11368-scene-representation-and-architecture.md) establishes the global mapping from 6D coordinates to radiance properties, these methods partition the domain to enable parallel training, reduce memory footprint, or handle dynamic content [G]. They differ from siblings like [Specific Model Architectures](8585-specific-model-architectures.md) by focusing on the structural decomposition of the scene space rather than the internal topology of the neural network itself [G].
+Addresses scalability limits by decomposing the scene into multiple smaller representations optimized independently. Sets itself apart by focusing on structural decomposition of the scene space rather than internal network topology. Choose for parallel training, reduced memory footprint, or dynamic content handling.
 
 ### [Specific Model Architectures and Extensions](10278-specific-model-architectures-and-extensions.md)
-This family represents a scene as a single MLP $f_\theta(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{d}) \to (\mathbf{c}, \sigma)$ that maps a 3D position $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and a viewing direction $\mathbf{d} \in S^2$ to an emitted color $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and a volumetric density $\sigma \ge 0$. Rendering uses the classical volume rendering integral along rays cast from the camera, with no explicit geometry—geometry emerges from where $\sigma$ is high after photometric optimization on multi-view images. It sets itself apart from its siblings by using a single, monolithic MLP without spatial partitioning or explicit geometric priors, relying entirely on positional encoding and hierarchical sampling to capture scene details. Unlike [Mip-NeRF 360](3306-mip-nerf-360.md) which handles unbounded scenes via contraction, or [NeRF-FF](8669-nerf-ff.md) which addresses defocus, the original architecture operates within defined near and far ray bounds.
+Represents a scene as a single MLP mapping 3D position and viewing direction to color and density. Sets itself apart by using a monolithic MLP without spatial partitioning or explicit geometric priors, relying on positional encoding and hierarchical sampling. Choose for standard unbounded scene representation within defined ray bounds.
 
 ### [NeRFReN](11981-nerfren.md)
-NeRFReN addresses the failure of standard Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) to render reflective surfaces, such as glass and mirrors, by separating the radiance field into transmitted and reflected components. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods by explicitly modeling the reflection fraction $\beta$ derived from the transmitted geometry, allowing for the separation of reflection artifacts from physical entities.
-
-### [Neural Body](12176-neural-body.md)
-Neural Body is a method for synthesizing novel views of dynamic human avatars from sparse multi-view videos by combining NeRF volume rendering with a vertex-based deformable human body model (SMPL). It solves the problem of rendering articulated bodies with moving poses, which standard NeRF approaches struggle with due to the complexity of modeling large non-rigid deformations. By anchoring latent variables to the vertices of a deformable mesh, Neural Body enables the interpolation of poses and frames, establishing a foundational paradigm for neural field rendering of human avatars.
-
-### [NeuMan](12480-neuman.md)
-NeuMan renders a human together with a scene with novel human poses and novel views from a single video by jointly optimizing a scene NeRF and a pose-driven human NeRF. It sets itself apart from standard NeRF variants by introducing a canonical space based on the Da-pose SMPL mesh to avoid volume collision during warping, and an error-correction network to handle details not expressed by the SMPL model. Unlike methods that require multi-view video or motion capture, NeuMan operates on a single monocular video sequence.
+Separates the radiance field into transmitted and reflected components to handle reflective surfaces like glass and mirrors. Sets itself apart by explicitly modeling the reflection fraction derived from transmitted geometry. Choose when rendering reflective materials is required.
 
 ### [AR-NeRF](12493-ar-nerf.md)
-AR-NeRF is an extension of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) that employs an aperture camera model instead of a pinhole camera model to represent defocus effects and viewpoint changes in a unified manner within a common ray-tracing framework. It enables fully unsupervised learning of depth and defocus effects from single natural images by jointly leveraging defocus and viewpoint cues without explicit supervision or pretrained models. Unlike standard NeRF, which assumes a pinhole camera, AR-NeRF represents each pixel using a collection of rays converging at a focus plane, allowing the model to disentangle defocus-aware and defocus-independent representations through aperture randomized training.
+Employs an aperture camera model to represent defocus effects and viewpoint changes. Sets itself apart by using a collection of rays converging at a focus plane per pixel, enabling unsupervised learning of depth and defocus. Choose for single-image defocus and depth estimation.
 
 ### [Neural Implicit Surfaces with PBR](13481-neural-implicit-surfaces-with-pbr.md)
-This method integrates a Signed Distance Function (SDF) with a Physically-Based Rendering (PBR) material model to achieve accurate scene representation, specifically separating specular and diffuse reflections. It addresses the limitation of standard NeRF variants in modeling mirror-like reflections by deriving smooth surface normals from the SDF gradient rather than optimizing them directly, and by using a precomputed BRDF LUT for specular calculation. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Ref-NeRF](11981-nerfren.md) by explicitly enforcing the Eikonal equation for geometric smoothness and using a standard PBR decomposition ($C_d + C_s$) rather than view-dependent color networks alone.
+Integrates a Signed Distance Function (SDF) with a Physically-Based Rendering (PBR) material model. Sets itself apart by deriving smooth normals from the SDF gradient and using a precomputed BRDF LUT for specular calculation. Choose for accurate mirror-like reflections and geometric smoothness.
+
+### [Human and Avatar Modeling](18785-human-and-avatar-modeling.md)
+Specializes in synthesizing novel views of dynamic human avatars from sparse inputs. Sets itself apart by combining NeRF volume rendering with deformable human body models (e.g., SMPL) to handle articulated poses. Choose for dynamic human rendering and pose interpolation.
 
 #### Surface-aware density formulation
-- **Definition:** A method to extract high-quality surfaces from NeRF by modeling geometry as a distance function or occupancy field and converting its output to density using a probability density function [S1].
-- **Step:** Incorporate an eikonal term to encourage geometric consistency, enforcing that the gradient norm of the learned SDF is equal to one [S1].
-- **Formula:** Density distribution: $\Phi_S(x) = \frac{se^{-sx}}{1+e^{-sx}}$, where $s$ is a scaling parameter and $x$ is the SDF or occupancy value [S1].
+A method to extract high-quality surfaces from NeRF by modeling geometry as a distance function or occupancy field and converting its output to density using a probability density function [S1]. It incorporates an eikonal term to encourage geometric consistency, enforcing that the gradient norm of the learned SDF is equal to one [S1]. The density distribution is defined as $\Phi_S(x) = \frac{se^{-sx}}{1+e^{-sx}}$, where $s$ is a scaling parameter and $x$ is the SDF or occupancy value [S1].
 
 #### NELF
-- **Definition:** A neural volumetric rendering framework modeling scene appearance using light transport vectors, enabling realistic relighting and view synthesis of human portraits from only five input images [S2].
-- **Step:** A UNet-style CNN extracts per-view features. An MLP regresses volume density and transport vectors. Environment maps are estimated to disentangle lighting. Trained on synthetic data and adapted to real images via a domain adaptation module [S2].
-
-#### NeRF with Per-Frame Latent Codes
-- **Definition:** A method using per-frame learned latent codes and adding facial expression as a 76-dimensional coefficient of a morphable model constructed from Face2Face [210] [S2].
+A neural volumetric rendering framework modeling scene appearance using light transport vectors, enabling realistic relighting and view synthesis of human portraits from only five input images [S2]. It uses a UNet-style CNN to extract per-view features, an MLP to regress volume density and transport vectors, and estimates environment maps to disentangle lighting [S2]. Trained on synthetic data and adapted to real images via a domain adaptation module, it achieves photo-realistic, lighting-consistent renderings [S2].
 
 #### Nerfacto Pipeline
-- **Definition:** The default baseline model in Nerfstudio, integrating several advances from recent NeRF literature into a single, efficient pipeline for in-the-wild reconstruction [S3, unofficial].
-- **Step:** Combine a hash-grid encoding for compact and expressive scene representation. Use Mip-NeRF 360 proposal sampling for efficient ray sampling. Apply distortion loss from Mip-NeRF 360 to stabilize convergence. Apply scene contraction to handle unbounded scenes. Apply camera poses refinement. Apply appearance embeddings to account for illumination variations across input images [S3, unofficial].
+The default baseline model in Nerfstudio, integrating several advances from recent NeRF literature into a single, efficient pipeline for in-the-wild reconstruction [S3, unofficial]. It combines a hash-grid encoding for compact and expressive scene representation, Mip-NeRF 360 proposal sampling for efficient ray sampling, distortion loss from Mip-NeRF 360 to stabilize convergence, scene contraction to handle unbounded scenes, camera poses refinement, and appearance embeddings to account for illumination variations across input images [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The canonical NeRF architecture was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. It established the paradigm of using a single MLP to map 6D coordinates to radiance properties. Subsequent developments addressed specific limitations: reflective surfaces (NeRFReN, Neural Implicit Surfaces with PBR) [S1], dynamic humans (Neural Body, NeuMan) [S1, S2], defocus (AR-NeRF) [S1], and scalability (Scene Representation and Partitioning, Nerfacto) [S1][S3, unofficial]. The field has evolved from monolithic MLPs to hybrid representations and specialized architectures for specific domains like human avatars and reflective materials.
